Joint pain and stiffness are common health concerns that can affect mobility, physical comfort and quality of life. A person may experience discomfort in one joint, such as the knee or shoulder, or in several joints across the body. Symptoms may develop after physical activity, follow an injury or appear without an obvious cause.
For some individuals, joint stiffness is most noticeable in the morning or after sitting for a long time. Others experience pain while walking, climbing stairs, lifting objects or performing routine household activities. The severity can range from mild discomfort to significant pain that interferes with daily movement.
Although joint pain is often associated with ageing, it does not affect only older adults. Younger people can develop joint problems because of sports injuries, infections, autoimmune conditions and inflammatory disorders. Persistent pain or stiffness should not automatically be dismissed as a normal consequence of getting older.
Understanding the underlying cause is important because different joint conditions require different approaches to treatment. Osteoarthritis, rheumatoid arthritis, gout, bursitis and joint infections may produce overlapping symptoms, but their management can be very different.
This guide explains the common causes of joint pain and stiffness, the symptoms that require medical attention, how joint conditions are diagnosed and the treatment and lifestyle measures that may help protect joint health.
What Are Joints and Why Do They Become Painful?
Joints are the points where two or more bones meet. They allow the body to bend, rotate, support weight and perform coordinated movements.
Healthy joints contain several structures that work together:
•	Cartilage: A smooth, protective tissue covering the ends of bones. It helps reduce friction during movement.
•	Synovial membrane: A thin lining inside certain joints that produces synovial fluid.
•	Synovial fluid: A lubricating fluid that helps joints move smoothly.
•	Ligaments: Strong bands of tissue that connect bones and provide stability.
•	Tendons: Tissues that connect muscles to bones and help produce movement.
•	Joint capsule: A surrounding structure that supports and encloses the joint.
Pain may develop when one or more of these structures becomes injured, inflamed, infected or damaged.
For example, cartilage changes may contribute to osteoarthritis, while inflammation of the joint lining is an important feature of rheumatoid arthritis. An injury can affect ligaments, tendons or cartilage, and an infection can cause rapid inflammation within the joint.
The location and nature of the discomfort can offer useful clues, but a medical assessment is needed to determine the actual cause.
What Is Joint Pain?
Joint pain, medically known as arthralgia, refers to discomfort or pain in an area where bones meet.
It may affect:
•	Knees.
•	Hips.
•	Shoulders.
•	Elbows.
•	Wrists.
•	Fingers.
•	Ankles.
•	Feet.
•	Spine-related joints.
Joint pain may be described as aching, sharp, burning, throbbing or tender. Some people experience discomfort only during movement, while others notice pain even when resting.
Joint pain can occur with or without visible swelling. It may also be accompanied by warmth, redness, reduced movement or a feeling that the joint is unstable.
It is important to distinguish pain originating inside a joint from discomfort in nearby muscles, tendons, ligaments or bursae. These structures can produce similar symptoms but may require different treatment.
What Is Joint Stiffness?
Joint stiffness refers to difficulty moving a joint through its normal range of motion.
A person may feel that a joint is tight, restricted or difficult to bend. Stiffness may be temporary or persistent and can occur with or without pain.
Common examples include:
•	Difficulty bending the fingers after waking up.
•	Knee stiffness after sitting for a long time.
•	Hip stiffness when getting out of a chair.
•	Shoulder restriction while reaching overhead.
•	Ankle stiffness when taking the first few steps in the morning.
The duration and timing of stiffness can be useful diagnostic clues.
For instance, brief stiffness after inactivity may occur with osteoarthritis. Prolonged morning stiffness involving several joints may suggest an inflammatory condition such as rheumatoid arthritis.
However, these patterns are not definitive. A healthcare professional considers the complete symptom history and examination findings before reaching a diagnosis.
Common Causes of Joint Pain and Stiffness
Joint pain has several possible causes. Some conditions involve gradual wear and structural changes, while others are associated with inflammation, infection or injury.
1. Osteoarthritis
Osteoarthritis is one of the most common causes of persistent joint pain, particularly in adults and older people.
It is a condition involving changes in the entire joint, including cartilage, underlying bone and surrounding tissues. Cartilage may become damaged, and the joint can develop structural changes over time.
Osteoarthritis commonly affects:
•	Knees.
•	Hips.
•	Hands and fingers.
•	Spine.
•	Base of the thumb.
Common symptoms include:
•	Pain during movement or weight-bearing activity.
•	Stiffness after periods of inactivity.
•	Reduced joint flexibility.
•	Mild swelling.
•	Tenderness around the joint.
•	A grinding or cracking sensation in some cases.
Morning stiffness associated with osteoarthritis is often relatively brief, although symptoms vary.
Risk factors include increasing age, previous joint injuries, family history, excess body weight and repetitive mechanical stress.
Osteoarthritis is not simply a result of bones rubbing together. It involves several changes in joint tissues, and its progression differs between individuals.
Treatment may include exercise, physiotherapy, weight management where appropriate, pain relief and other medical interventions depending on symptom severity.
2. Rheumatoid Arthritis
Rheumatoid arthritis is a chronic autoimmune inflammatory disease.
In this condition, the immune system mistakenly attacks the lining of joints, leading to inflammation. Over time, untreated inflammation can damage cartilage, bone and surrounding structures.
Rheumatoid arthritis commonly affects the small joints of the hands and feet, although larger joints can also become involved.
Typical symptoms include:
•	Pain and swelling in several joints.
•	Morning stiffness that may last an hour or longer.
•	Warmth around affected joints.
•	Tenderness when pressing the joints.
•	Reduced grip strength.
•	Fatigue.
•	General feelings of illness in some individuals.
The condition often affects joints on both sides of the body, although this pattern is not present in every case.
Rheumatoid arthritis may also affect organs beyond the joints, including the eyes, lungs and cardiovascular system.
Early diagnosis and treatment are important because controlling inflammation can reduce the risk of permanent joint damage.
Treatment commonly involves disease-modifying antirheumatic drugs, often referred to as DMARDs, along with monitoring and supportive care.
3. Gout
Gout is an inflammatory form of arthritis caused by the formation of monosodium urate crystals in and around joints.
These crystals develop when uric acid levels remain high enough for crystals to form. However, not everyone with elevated uric acid develops gout.
A gout attack may begin suddenly, sometimes during the night, and cause intense pain.
Common features include:
•	Sudden severe joint pain.
•	Redness.
•	Swelling.
•	Warmth.
•	Extreme tenderness.
•	Difficulty moving the affected joint.
The big toe is a classic site of gout, but the ankles, knees, wrists and fingers may also be affected.
Gout can occur in repeated episodes. Some people develop long-term inflammation and deposits of urate crystals called tophi if the condition remains uncontrolled.
Factors associated with gout include genetics, kidney function, certain medicines, alcohol consumption and dietary patterns.
Diagnosis may involve blood tests and, when necessary, examination of joint fluid for urate crystals.
Treatment of an acute attack differs from long-term management. A doctor may prescribe anti-inflammatory medicines for an attack and urate-lowering treatment for selected patients to prevent future episodes.
4. Joint Injuries and Sports-Related Problems
Joint injuries are common among people who participate in sports, exercise or physically demanding activities.
They may also occur after falls, accidents or sudden twisting movements.
Examples include:
•	Ligament sprains.
•	Meniscus injuries in the knee.
•	Cartilage injuries.
•	Shoulder dislocations.
•	Tendon injuries.
•	Joint fractures.
Symptoms may include sudden pain, swelling, bruising, reduced movement and difficulty putting weight on the affected limb.
A sprain involves stretching or tearing of a ligament. A strain generally affects a muscle or tendon.
Some injuries cause immediate symptoms, while others become more noticeable over several hours.
Severe pain, visible deformity, inability to bear weight or significant swelling after an injury requires prompt medical assessment.
5. Bursitis
Bursae are small, fluid-filled sacs that reduce friction between bones, tendons and muscles.
Bursitis occurs when a bursa becomes irritated or inflamed.
It commonly affects the:
•	Shoulder.
•	Elbow.
•	Hip.
•	Knee.
•	Heel area.
Symptoms may include localised pain, tenderness, swelling and discomfort during movement.
Bursitis can develop because of repetitive pressure, prolonged kneeling, repetitive movement, injury or certain inflammatory conditions.
For example, repeatedly leaning on the elbows may irritate the bursa around the elbow.
Treatment depends on the cause and may include activity modification, physiotherapy, suitable pain relief and other interventions recommended by a clinician.
6. Tendinitis and Tendinopathy
Tendons connect muscles to bones and help the body move.
Tendinitis generally refers to tendon inflammation, while tendinopathy is a broader term describing tendon pain and changes in tendon structure.
These conditions can cause pain near a joint and may be mistaken for joint pain itself.
Common areas include:
•	Shoulder.
•	Elbow.
•	Wrist.
•	Knee.
•	Achilles tendon near the ankle.
Symptoms often include pain during movement, tenderness and difficulty performing activities that involve the affected tendon.
Repetitive activity, sudden increases in exercise intensity and mechanical overload may contribute.
Treatment often involves adjusting activities and following a structured rehabilitation programme.
7. Septic Arthritis: Joint Infection
Septic arthritis is an infection inside a joint, usually caused by bacteria.
It is a potentially serious condition because infection can damage cartilage and surrounding joint structures rapidly.
Common symptoms include:
•	Sudden severe joint pain.
•	Significant swelling.
•	Warmth and redness.
•	Fever or chills.
•	Difficulty moving the joint.
•	Inability to bear weight in some cases.
The knee is commonly affected, but other joints can also become infected.
Septic arthritis may occur when microorganisms enter the joint through the bloodstream, an injury, surgery or another route.
People with weakened immune systems, existing joint disease, recent joint surgery or certain medical conditions may have increased risk.
Suspected septic arthritis requires urgent medical assessment. Treatment usually involves antibiotics and drainage of infected joint fluid, depending on the clinical situation.
Do not wait for symptoms to settle at home if a joint becomes suddenly hot, swollen and severely painful, particularly when fever is present.
8. Viral Infections and Joint Pain
Some viral infections can cause temporary joint pain and stiffness.
The symptoms may occur during the infection or after the initial illness.
Depending on the virus, a person may experience:
•	Pain in several joints.
•	Muscle aches.
•	Fever.
•	Fatigue.
•	Skin rash.
•	Temporary swelling.
Certain viral illnesses can cause prolonged joint symptoms in some individuals.
The pattern depends on the infection, and medical evaluation may be appropriate when joint pain is severe, persistent or associated with other symptoms.
9. Psoriatic Arthritis
Psoriatic arthritis is an inflammatory joint disease associated with psoriasis, a skin condition that causes patches of red, scaly skin.
Some people develop joint symptoms before visible psoriasis appears.
Possible features include:
•	Joint pain and swelling.
•	Morning stiffness.
•	Swelling of an entire finger or toe.
•	Heel pain.
•	Nail pitting or nail separation.
•	Back pain or stiffness.
Swelling of an entire finger or toe is known as dactylitis and can be an important clinical clue.
Psoriatic arthritis can affect different joints and may lead to permanent damage if inflammation is not adequately controlled.
Early assessment by a rheumatologist can help establish the diagnosis and guide treatment.
10. Ankylosing Spondylitis and Axial Spondyloarthritis
Axial spondyloarthritis is an inflammatory condition that mainly affects the spine and joints connecting the spine to the pelvis.
Ankylosing spondylitis is a form of axial spondyloarthritis in which structural changes may become visible on imaging.
Common symptoms include:
•	Persistent lower back pain.
•	Morning stiffness.
•	Pain that improves with movement.
•	Discomfort after prolonged rest.
•	Pain around the buttocks or hips.
•	Reduced spinal flexibility.
Unlike many mechanical causes of back pain, inflammatory back pain may improve with activity rather than rest.
Some individuals also experience eye inflammation, heel pain or other inflammatory symptoms.
Early diagnosis is important because appropriate treatment can help control inflammation and preserve function.
11. Lupus and Other Autoimmune Conditions
Systemic lupus erythematosus, commonly called lupus, is an autoimmune condition that can affect several parts of the body.
Joint pain and stiffness are common symptoms, and some individuals experience swelling in multiple joints.
Other possible features include:
•	Fatigue.
•	Skin rashes.
•	Mouth ulcers.
•	Sensitivity to sunlight.
•	Fever.
•	Hair loss.
•	Kidney-related symptoms.
Not everyone with lupus experiences the same symptoms.
Other autoimmune and connective tissue conditions may also cause joint pain. Diagnosis depends on clinical assessment and carefully selected investigations.
12. Fibromyalgia
Fibromyalgia is a chronic pain condition associated with widespread musculoskeletal pain, fatigue and sleep disturbances.
People with fibromyalgia may describe stiffness, tenderness and discomfort around joints, muscles and other soft tissues.
Other symptoms can include:
•	Poor sleep.
•	Difficulty concentrating.
•	Headaches.
•	Sensitivity to touch.
•	Persistent tiredness.
Fibromyalgia does not usually cause the joint swelling and structural damage associated with inflammatory arthritis.
A medical assessment helps distinguish fibromyalgia from inflammatory, neurological and other musculoskeletal conditions.
13. Osteoporosis and Fractures
Osteoporosis is a condition in which bones become weaker and more vulnerable to fractures.
It does not usually cause joint pain directly. However, fractures involving bones near joints or compression fractures in the spine can cause significant pain and movement difficulties.
Older adults and people with certain medical conditions may have an increased risk.
Sudden pain after a minor fall or an unexpected loss of mobility should be assessed, particularly in someone with known osteoporosis.
14. Repetitive Strain and Overuse
Repeated movements can place stress on joints and surrounding tissues.
Examples include:
•	Repeated typing or gripping.
•	Frequent lifting.
•	Long periods of kneeling.
•	Repetitive overhead activity.
•	Sudden increases in exercise.
•	Prolonged manual work.
Overuse may contribute to tendon irritation, bursitis or other musculoskeletal problems.
Symptoms often develop gradually and may worsen during or after the activity that triggers them.
Adjusting the activity, improving technique and allowing adequate recovery can help reduce ongoing strain.
Joint Pain in Different Parts of the Body
The location of joint pain can help guide medical assessment.
Knee Pain and Stiffness
Knee pain is common and may develop due to osteoarthritis, ligament injuries, meniscus damage, bursitis or inflammatory arthritis.
Symptoms can include pain while walking, difficulty climbing stairs, swelling, stiffness after sitting and reduced ability to bend the knee.
A knee that suddenly becomes very swollen, hot or severely painful needs prompt assessment.
Hip Pain and Stiffness
Hip pain may originate inside the joint or from surrounding muscles, tendons and bursae.
Common causes include osteoarthritis, bursitis, tendon problems and injuries.
Hip joint pain may be felt in the groin, thigh or buttock. It can make walking, standing and getting out of a chair difficult.
Shoulder Pain and Restricted Movement
Shoulder discomfort may result from rotator cuff problems, bursitis, arthritis or previous injuries.
People may notice difficulty reaching overhead, lifting objects or reaching behind their back.
Sudden severe pain after an injury, visible deformity or inability to move the arm requires medical evaluation.
Finger and Wrist Pain
Pain in the fingers and wrists can be associated with rheumatoid arthritis, osteoarthritis, gout, tendon disorders or repetitive strain.
Morning stiffness, swelling of several finger joints and reduced grip strength may suggest inflammatory arthritis.
A doctor may examine the small joints and assess whether symptoms are symmetrical.
Ankle and Foot Pain
Ankle and foot pain can result from injuries, osteoarthritis, gout, inflammatory arthritis and tendon disorders.
Sudden severe pain in the big toe may be associated with gout, while swelling after a twisting injury may indicate a sprain or fracture.
Persistent foot or ankle pain can affect walking and should be evaluated when it does not improve.
Morning Joint Stiffness: What Does It Mean?
Morning joint stiffness is an important symptom to describe during a medical consultation.
Some people experience stiffness for a few minutes after waking up. Others find that movement remains restricted for a much longer period.
Brief stiffness may occur with osteoarthritis or after prolonged inactivity.
Persistent morning stiffness, particularly when several joints are swollen or painful, may suggest an inflammatory condition such as rheumatoid arthritis.
However, duration alone cannot confirm a diagnosis.
A healthcare professional will also consider:
•	Which joints are affected.
•	Whether symptoms occur on both sides.
•	Whether swelling is present.
•	Whether movement improves symptoms.
•	Whether fatigue or fever occurs.
•	How long the symptoms have been present.
Keeping a record of morning stiffness can help the doctor understand the pattern.
Risk Factors That May Increase the Likelihood of Joint Problems
Joint pain can affect anyone, but certain factors may increase the likelihood of developing specific joint conditions.
Increasing Age
The risk of osteoarthritis increases with age, partly because joint tissues undergo changes over time.
However, joint pain in older adults should still be evaluated rather than automatically attributed to ageing.
Previous Joint Injuries
A previous fracture, ligament injury or cartilage injury can increase the risk of developing later joint problems.
Appropriate rehabilitation and follow-up may help reduce avoidable complications.
Excess Body Weight
Excess body weight can increase mechanical stress on weight-bearing joints, particularly the knees and hips.
Fat tissue also has biological activity that may influence inflammation.
Weight management, when appropriate, can help reduce joint stress and support overall health.
Family History and Genetics
Some joint conditions have a genetic component.
Family history may influence the risk of osteoarthritis, rheumatoid arthritis, gout and certain autoimmune conditions.
Repetitive Physical Activity
Certain occupations and sports involve repeated movements or prolonged pressure on particular joints.
Poor technique, inadequate recovery and sudden increases in activity may contribute to overuse problems.
Existing Medical Conditions
Diabetes, autoimmune diseases, kidney disease and certain infections can influence joint health or increase the risk of specific joint disorders.
A complete medical history helps identify relevant risk factors.
When Should You Seek Medical Help for Joint Pain?
Not all joint pain requires emergency treatment. However, persistent, worsening or unexplained symptoms should be assessed.
Arrange a medical consultation if:
•	Joint pain lasts for several days without improvement.
•	Pain repeatedly returns.
•	Stiffness affects everyday activities.
•	Several joints become painful.
•	Joint swelling develops.
•	Morning stiffness is prolonged.
•	Movement becomes increasingly restricted.
•	Pain interferes with sleep.
•	There is unexplained fatigue or weight loss.
•	Joint symptoms occur with a skin rash or other systemic symptoms.
Early assessment can help identify conditions that may benefit from prompt treatment.
Warning Signs That Need Urgent Medical Attention
Certain symptoms may indicate a serious condition.
Seek urgent medical care if a joint becomes:
•	Suddenly and severely painful.
•	Hot, red and swollen.
•	Difficult or impossible to move.
•	Painful alongside fever or chills.
•	Swollen after a significant injury.
•	Deformed after a fall or accident.
A sudden hot, swollen joint may indicate septic arthritis, even if fever is absent.
After an injury, urgent assessment is also important if there is severe pain, an obvious deformity, numbness, loss of circulation or inability to bear weight.
Do not delay care for a suspected joint infection or serious injury.
How Are Joint Pain and Stiffness Diagnosed?
A diagnosis is based on the combination of medical history, physical examination and investigations when needed.
There is no single test that identifies every cause of joint pain.
1. Medical History
The doctor may ask:
•	When did the pain begin?
•	Did it start suddenly or gradually?
•	Which joints are affected?
•	Is the pain present during rest or movement?
•	How long does morning stiffness last?
•	Is there visible swelling?
•	Has there been a recent injury?
•	Are there fever, fatigue or skin changes?
•	Is there a family history of arthritis?
•	Are any medicines being taken?
•	Has there been a recent infection?
The answers help distinguish mechanical, inflammatory, infectious and other causes.
2. Physical Examination
The doctor may examine the affected joints for:
•	Swelling.
•	Redness.
•	Warmth.
•	Tenderness.
•	Range of movement.
•	Joint stability.
•	Deformity.
•	Muscle strength.
•	Pain during specific movements.
The examination may also include assessment of the surrounding muscles, tendons and ligaments.
3. Blood Tests
Blood tests may be recommended when inflammatory, autoimmune, infectious or metabolic conditions are suspected.
Possible investigations include:
Complete Blood Count
A complete blood count can help identify anaemia and changes in blood cells that may occur with certain infections or inflammatory conditions.
ESR and CRP
Erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein are markers that may indicate inflammation.
They can support the assessment of inflammatory conditions but are not specific to one disease.
Rheumatoid Factor
Rheumatoid factor may be present in some people with rheumatoid arthritis, but it can also occur in other conditions and in some healthy individuals.
A positive result alone does not confirm rheumatoid arthritis.
Anti-CCP Antibodies
Anti-cyclic citrullinated peptide antibodies can help support the diagnosis of rheumatoid arthritis when interpreted alongside symptoms and examination findings.
Serum Uric Acid
Uric acid testing may help assess gout risk. However, a normal result during an acute attack does not rule out gout, and an elevated result alone does not confirm it.
Other Tests
Depending on symptoms, the doctor may consider kidney function, liver function, thyroid tests, autoimmune investigations or tests for particular infections.
Not everyone with joint pain needs an extensive blood-test panel.
4. Imaging Tests
Imaging can help assess joint structures and identify injuries or degenerative changes.
X-rays
X-rays may show joint-space narrowing, bone changes, fractures and certain forms of arthritis.
Ultrasound
Ultrasound can help assess joint swelling, fluid accumulation, inflammation and some tendon or bursa problems.
MRI
Magnetic resonance imaging provides detailed images of soft tissues, cartilage, ligaments and other structures.
It may be useful when an injury or internal joint problem is suspected and other investigations are insufficient.
CT Scans
Computed tomography may be used to assess complex fractures or selected bone and joint conditions.
The choice of imaging depends on the clinical question. An MRI is not necessary for every person with joint pain.
5. Joint Fluid Analysis
If a joint is significantly swollen, a doctor may recommend arthrocentesis, also called joint aspiration.
A needle is used to remove a small amount of synovial fluid for analysis.
The fluid may be examined for:
•	White blood cell count.
•	Bacteria or other microorganisms.
•	Urate crystals.
•	Calcium pyrophosphate crystals.
•	Other signs of inflammation.
Joint fluid analysis can be particularly important when septic arthritis or crystal-related arthritis is suspected.
Treatment Options for Joint Pain and Stiffness
Treatment depends on the underlying cause, severity of symptoms, affected joint and the individual's general health.
A treatment that is appropriate for osteoarthritis may not be suitable for septic arthritis or rheumatoid arthritis.
1. Rest and Activity Modification
Short-term modification of activities may help when pain develops after overuse or injury.
However, prolonged complete rest can lead to muscle weakness and reduced joint mobility.
The goal is usually to avoid activities that worsen symptoms while maintaining safe, appropriate movement.
2. Cold and Warm Compresses
Cold packs may help reduce discomfort and swelling after some injuries or during an acute inflammatory episode.
Warm compresses may help relieve stiffness and make movement more comfortable for some people.
Use a protective layer between the pack and the skin, and avoid applying extreme temperatures directly.
People with reduced skin sensation or circulation problems should seek advice before using heat or cold therapy.
3. Pain-Relief Medicines
Depending on the cause, a doctor may recommend paracetamol or non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs).
NSAIDs can help reduce pain and inflammation, but they may not be suitable for everyone.
Potential risks include stomach irritation, gastrointestinal bleeding, kidney problems, increased blood pressure and cardiovascular complications.
Topical NSAID preparations may be considered for certain joint conditions, particularly localized osteoarthritis.
Medication choice should account for age, pregnancy status, existing medical conditions and other medicines being taken.
4. Physiotherapy
Physiotherapy can help improve movement, strength, flexibility and joint function.
A physiotherapist may recommend:
•	Range-of-motion exercises.
•	Strengthening exercises.
•	Balance training.
•	Stretching.
•	Gait training.
•	Activity modification.
•	Joint-protection techniques.
The programme should be tailored to the diagnosis and the person's abilities.
5. Treatment for Rheumatoid and Other Inflammatory Arthritis
Inflammatory arthritis often requires specialist treatment.
For rheumatoid arthritis, DMARDs are used to control immune-driven inflammation and reduce the risk of joint damage.
Medicines may include conventional synthetic DMARDs, biologic therapies or targeted synthetic treatments.
These medicines require appropriate monitoring and follow-up.
Treatment for psoriatic arthritis and axial spondyloarthritis may also involve disease-modifying or targeted anti-inflammatory therapies.
6. Treatment for Gout
Acute gout attacks may be treated with medicines such as NSAIDs, colchicine or corticosteroids, depending on individual suitability.
For people with recurrent attacks or other indications, urate-lowering treatment may be prescribed to reduce uric acid levels and prevent crystal formation.
Long-term treatment requires monitoring and an individualized management plan.
7. Antibiotics and Joint Drainage for Infection
Septic arthritis is a medical emergency.
Treatment generally involves urgent joint fluid sampling, antibiotics and drainage of infected fluid.
Some patients require surgery to clear the infection, depending on the joint and clinical circumstances.
Treatment should be provided in a hospital or appropriate specialist setting.
8. Joint Injections
In selected conditions, a doctor may consider an injection into or around a joint.
Depending on the diagnosis, this may involve a corticosteroid or another treatment.
Injections are not suitable for every type of joint pain. They should be considered only after an appropriate assessment, particularly when infection is a possibility.
9. Surgery and Joint Replacement
Surgery may be considered when structural damage or severe symptoms significantly affect quality of life and non-surgical treatment has not provided adequate relief.
Possible procedures include:
•	Arthroscopy for selected joint problems.
•	Ligament reconstruction.
•	Fracture repair.
•	Joint replacement.
Hip and knee replacement may be considered for advanced osteoarthritis in appropriate patients.
The decision depends on symptoms, imaging, general health, expected benefits and surgical risks.
Lifestyle Habits That Support Joint Health
Lifestyle measures can complement medical treatment and help maintain mobility.
Maintain a Healthy Body Weight
For people carrying excess body weight, gradual weight management may reduce stress on weight-bearing joints.
Even modest changes can be helpful for some individuals with knee osteoarthritis.
Weight management should be approached safely, without extreme diets or unnecessary restrictions.
Stay Physically Active
Regular movement helps maintain muscle strength, flexibility and general fitness.
Low-impact activities may be suitable for many people with joint problems.
Examples include:
•	Walking.
•	Swimming.
•	Water-based exercise.
•	Cycling.
•	Gentle strengthening exercises.
•	Appropriate mobility exercises.
The best activity depends on the affected joint and the underlying condition.
Strengthen the Muscles Around Joints
Strong muscles help support joints and improve movement control.
A structured exercise programme may reduce pain and improve function in conditions such as osteoarthritis.
Exercise should be introduced gradually, particularly after an injury or a period of inactivity.
Eat a Balanced Diet
A balanced diet supports general health, bone health and muscle function.
Include suitable sources of:
•	Protein.
•	Calcium.
•	Vitamin D.
•	Fruits and vegetables.
•	Whole grains.
•	Healthy fats.
People with gout may benefit from discussing dietary patterns and alcohol intake with their doctor.
No single food or supplement can cure arthritis.
Avoid Smoking
Smoking is associated with several adverse health outcomes and may worsen the course of certain inflammatory conditions, including rheumatoid arthritis.
Stopping smoking supports overall health and may improve treatment outcomes.
Prioritize Adequate Sleep
Pain can interfere with sleep, while poor sleep may make pain harder to manage.
A regular sleep schedule and appropriate treatment of the underlying joint condition may help improve rest.
Can Joint Pain and Stiffness Be Prevented?
Not all joint conditions can be prevented. Genetic, autoimmune and age-related factors cannot always be controlled.
However, several habits may help reduce avoidable joint stress and injury:
•	Maintain regular physical activity.
•	Build strength gradually.
•	Use correct exercise techniques.
•	Avoid sudden increases in training intensity.
•	Wear suitable footwear for activities.
•	Use appropriate protective equipment during sports.
•	Manage body weight where appropriate.
•	Seek treatment for joint injuries.
•	Avoid prolonged repetitive strain.
•	Follow medical advice for chronic conditions.
Early attention to recurring joint symptoms may also help prevent complications in conditions where timely treatment is important.
Common Myths About Joint Pain
Myth 1: Joint Pain Is Always a Sign of Ageing
Joint pain can occur at any age. Injuries, infections, autoimmune diseases and inflammatory conditions can affect children, teenagers and young adults as well as older people.
Myth 2: Cracking Joints Always Causes Arthritis
Ordinary joint cracking or popping does not necessarily mean that arthritis is developing. However, cracking accompanied by pain, swelling or reduced movement should be assessed.
Myth 3: Complete Rest Is Best for Every Joint Problem
Rest may be helpful during an acute injury or flare, but prolonged inactivity can reduce strength and mobility. Appropriate movement is often an important part of recovery.
Myth 4: All Arthritis Is the Same
Osteoarthritis, rheumatoid arthritis, gout and psoriatic arthritis have different causes and treatment approaches. Accurate diagnosis is important.
Myth 5: Joint Pain Can Always Be Managed With Painkillers
Painkillers may reduce symptoms, but they do not treat every underlying cause. Some conditions require antibiotics, disease-modifying medicines, rehabilitation or surgery.
Myth 6: Supplements Can Reverse Joint Damage
No supplement has been proven to reverse all forms of joint damage. Supplements should not replace appropriate medical treatment.
Frequently Asked Questions About Joint Pain and Stiffness
1. What are the most common causes of joint pain?
Common causes include osteoarthritis, rheumatoid arthritis, gout, sports injuries, bursitis, tendinopathy and inflammatory conditions. Joint infections are less common but require urgent attention.
2. Why do my joints feel stiff in the morning?
Morning stiffness may occur after prolonged inactivity or because of inflammation within the joints. Brief stiffness can occur with osteoarthritis, while prolonged stiffness affecting several joints may suggest inflammatory arthritis.
3. Is joint pain always related to arthritis?
No. Joint pain can also result from injuries, tendon problems, bursitis, infections and other musculoskeletal conditions.
4. What is the difference between osteoarthritis and rheumatoid arthritis?
Osteoarthritis involves structural changes in the entire joint, including cartilage and underlying bone. Rheumatoid arthritis is an autoimmune inflammatory condition that primarily affects the joint lining and can damage joints over time.
5. Can young adults develop joint pain?
Yes. Young adults can develop joint pain because of sports injuries, repetitive strain, autoimmune conditions, infections and other medical problems.
6. Why do my knees hurt when climbing stairs?
Knee pain during stair climbing may be associated with osteoarthritis, patellofemoral pain, tendon problems or other knee conditions. The cause depends on the location of pain and examination findings.
7. Can stress cause joint pain?
Stress does not usually cause structural joint damage, but it can influence pain perception, muscle tension and sleep. Persistent joint symptoms still need appropriate assessment.
8. Does cold weather cause joint stiffness?
Some people report increased joint discomfort or stiffness in cold weather. However, weather changes do not explain every case of joint pain and do not necessarily indicate worsening joint damage.
9. Can dehydration cause joint pain?
Dehydration may contribute to general discomfort and affect overall health, but it is not a common explanation for persistent joint pain. Ongoing symptoms should be assessed for other causes.
10. What are the warning signs of rheumatoid arthritis?
Persistent swelling and pain in several joints, prolonged morning stiffness, tenderness in the small joints of the hands and feet, and fatigue may suggest rheumatoid arthritis. Medical evaluation is important for diagnosis.
11. How can I tell whether joint pain is due to gout?
Gout often causes sudden, intense pain with redness, warmth and swelling, commonly in the big toe. Other joints can also be affected. Joint fluid analysis may be needed to confirm the diagnosis.
12. When is joint pain an emergency?
A suddenly hot, swollen and severely painful joint, particularly with fever or chills, may indicate septic arthritis and requires urgent medical care. Severe injuries with deformity or inability to bear weight also need prompt assessment.
13. Which doctor should I consult for joint pain?
A primary care doctor can begin the evaluation. Depending on the suspected cause, referral may be made to a rheumatologist, orthopaedic specialist or other appropriate clinician.
14. Are blood tests necessary for joint pain?
Not always. Blood tests may be recommended when the doctor suspects inflammation, autoimmune disease, infection or metabolic conditions. The choice depends on symptoms and examination findings.
15. Can exercise make joint pain better?
Appropriate exercise can improve strength, flexibility and joint function. However, the type and intensity should match the underlying condition. Pain that becomes severe or progressively worse during exercise should be assessed.
16. Is walking good for knee osteoarthritis?
Walking may be beneficial for many people with knee osteoarthritis when adjusted to their ability. A gradual programme and appropriate footwear may help. A physiotherapist can advise on suitable activity levels.
17. Can joint stiffness be cured?
Some causes of joint stiffness are temporary and improve with treatment. Chronic conditions may require long-term management. The outcome depends on the underlying diagnosis and extent of joint damage.
18. Does excess body weight affect joint health?
Excess body weight can increase mechanical stress on weight-bearing joints, particularly the knees and hips. Weight management may help reduce symptoms in some individuals with osteoarthritis.
19. Can joint pain be caused by an infection?
Yes. Viral infections can cause temporary joint symptoms, while bacterial infection inside a joint can cause septic arthritis. A hot, swollen and severely painful joint requires urgent assessment.
20. What is the best treatment for joint pain and stiffness?
There is no single best treatment for everyone. Management may involve physiotherapy, appropriate exercise, medication, weight management, treatment of inflammation or infection, injections or surgery depending on the cause.
Conclusion
Joint pain and stiffness can develop for many reasons, ranging from temporary overuse and injuries to chronic inflammatory diseases and degenerative joint conditions.
Osteoarthritis, rheumatoid arthritis, gout, bursitis, tendinopathy and joint infections can produce overlapping symptoms, but they require different approaches to diagnosis and treatment.
Paying attention to the location, duration and pattern of joint symptoms can help identify when medical assessment is needed. Persistent swelling, prolonged morning stiffness, unexplained pain and reduced mobility should not be ignored.
A suddenly hot, swollen and severely painful joint is particularly important because it may indicate a joint infection that requires urgent treatment.
Medical evaluation may involve a physical examination, selected blood tests, imaging or joint fluid analysis. Once the underlying cause is identified, an appropriate treatment plan can help relieve symptoms, improve movement and protect joint function.
Regular physical activity, suitable muscle strengthening, balanced nutrition and healthy weight management can support joint health. However, lifestyle measures should complement rather than replace medical care when an underlying condition is present.
The most effective way to manage joint pain is to understand its cause, seek help at the right time and follow a treatment plan suited to individual health needs.
